---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';
import Hale from '../components/Hale.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
const effectDiagnostic = `effect assertion violated:
  price must not reach syscall

reachable path:
  price
  -> Book.price_for
  -> Book.reload
  -> std::io::fs::read_file`;

const dependencyDiagnostic = `declared dependency set violated:
  StatedCarry can transitively depend on SumLookup

Path:
  subject SumLookup
  -> Launderer
  -> subject Recalled
  -> StatedCarry`;

const fleetWitness = `fleet claim \`orders_pass_oms\` violated — witness:
  prober-0::Probe::submit  [prober/main.hl]
  -(route \`intent\`)->
  oms-0::Oms::on_intent  [oms/main.hl]
  -(route \`bypass\`)->
  gw-0::Gateway::on_order  [gw/main.hl]`;

const claimDiagnostic = `claim \`iso_dg\` violated:
  \`delta_wing\` reaches \`gamma_wing\`

witness:
  delta::Triage::on_task
  -(publishes "org.metrics")->
  gamma::Research::on_metric`;

const commands = `hale check .
hale check . --dump-topology
hale test .
hale verify .
hale bench .
hale doc --stdlib
hale lsp
hale mcp`;
---
<Base title="What is checked" description="Every property the Hale compiler checks, who checks it, and the witness that comes back when it is false.">
  <Field />
  <section class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>What is checked, and what comes back when it fails</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Each property below is checked as an error.
        A property that is false gets a witness back from the compiler, the concrete path
        that breaks the rule, in your own names: the call, the route, and the
        places you would edit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">who checks</th><th scope="col">what</th><th scope="col">how</th><th scope="col"></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>Typed bus topology</td><td class="wrap">Topic declarations fix the payload type and the publish and subscribe authority. The compiler inspects the closed-world graph for orphaned subjects, impossible handlers, routing-policy errors and re-entrant cycles.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/services/bus">bus →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>Effect reachability</td><td class="wrap"><code>@no_syscall</code>, <code>@no_block</code>, <code>@deterministic</code> and <code>@budget</code> are checked across reachable calls, imported seeds, handles and interface dispatch.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/effects">effects →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>Causal bus contracts</td><td class="wrap"><code>causes:</code> follows outward influence across publishes. <code>depends:</code> walks backward through subscriptions and republishers to check the complete set that may reach a locus over the bus graph, including through a republisher in another library. Influence that travels outside the bus is not part of that closure.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/effects">causality →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>System claims</td><td class="wrap">Groups name the domains; the main locus states the law: <code>forbid reaches</code>, <code>only edges</code>, <code>require</code>, <code>cover</code>, <code>count</code>, interposition with <code>avoiding</code>, and capability bounds. Violations are errors with countermodels, and the checked model exports as a diffable artifact under a shape hash.</td><td class="go"><a href="/articles/claims-in-hale">claims →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>One claimset, many worlds</td><td class="wrap">A claim is evaluated in a closed world, so law used to be copied into every entry point. A <code>constitution</code> moves the authorship without moving the evaluation: each adopting main still proves it in its own world. Composition only adds. Identity follows the normalized closure, so a shared name is not mistaken for a shared claimset.</td><td class="go"><a href="/articles/constitutions-in-hale">constitutions →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>Across binaries</td><td class="wrap">Sealed application artifacts compose into one deployment model. A plan names deployed instances and the routes between them, and law that no single binary can state is checked over the arrangement. Composition takes artifacts, never merged source. Matching wire identities establish compatibility, and only an explicit route creates an edge.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/services/multi-binary">binaries →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">compiler</td><td>State a caller cannot reach</td><td class="wrap">A locus marked <code>@sealed</code> keeps its params to itself: others may call it and cannot read or write it. Loci are not otherwise encapsulated at the field level, so this is what makes confinement a fact the compiler knows. The library's key holders take the name of a source and never the bytes, so no line of application code holds the material. The check covers confinement and leaves information flow out: a value derived from the key is not tracked, and a constant-time compare still lets the verdict be published. Both limits are stated in the specification.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/verification#secrets-confine-classify-claim">secrets →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">runtime + compiler</td><td>Structural lifetime</td><td class="wrap">Loci own regions and children. The compiler enforces the managed-entity rules; the runtime dissolves the owned structure deterministically and reports residency when growth needs inspection.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/systems/memory">memory →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">runtime</td><td>Supervised failure</td><td class="wrap">Lifecycle failures travel to an owning parent. A binding that fails to start refuses birth, so no route is installed that accepts and drops messages.</td><td class="go"><a href="/docs/services/failure">failure →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="who">ci</td><td>Concurrent substrate</td><td class="wrap">Models of the runtime's synchronization protocols are model-checked under permitted thread interleavings in CI. This narrows the trusted base beneath queues, mailboxes, maps and arenas, without emptying it.</td><td class="go"><a href="#substrate">the models →</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <div class="witness" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <div>
        <h2>The diagnostic names the call path.</h2>
        <p>A hard rule is useful only when a rejection shows how the forbidden behaviour became reachable.</p>
      </div>
      <Hale code={effectDiagnostic} title="hale check" kind="effect witness" lang="text" />
    </div>

    <div class="witness">
      <div>
        <h2>The path continues through the broker.</h2>
        <p>Local inspection cannot see a dependency hidden behind a republisher. The bus graph can, and the witness names every step.</p>
      </div>
      <Hale code={dependencyDiagnostic} title="hale check" kind="dependency witness" lang="text" />
    </div>

    <div class="witness">
      <div>
        <h2>The law names its countermodel.</h2>
        <p>A claim quantifies over every represented path. A graph that escapes its declared law gets a diagnostic with the route, calls and message hops, that makes the sentence false.</p>
      </div>
      <Hale code={claimDiagnostic} title="hale check" kind="claim witness" lang="text" />
    </div>

    <div class="witness">
      <div>
        <h2>The path is named across binaries.</h2>
        <p>The same sentence, one scale out. Each component checked clean on its own; the arrangement did not. The witness crosses artifacts and names every hop, the route that carries it, and the file each vertex lives in, because a deployment defect is only actionable if it says which config and which source to open.</p>
      </div>
      <Hale code={fleetWitness} title="hale fleet check" kind="fleet witness" lang="text" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">The four answers a check can give</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="states">
      <div class="ok"><b>Proven</b><span>The build may continue.</span></div>
      <div class="bad"><b>Violated</b><span>Fail, with a witness.</span></div>
      <div class="unk"><b>Unknown</b><span>Fail closed, for a hard contract.</span></div>
      <div><b>Unsupported</b><span>Say exactly what cannot be checked.</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="witness" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <div>
        <h2>The checks ship with the language.</h2>
        <p>The command line, editor integration, CI and the MCP server all read the compiler's structured diagnostics. The quickest evaluation is to run the examples and make the compiler disagree with you.</p>
      </div>
      <Terminal code={commands} title="toolchain" kind="commands" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose" id="substrate" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What stays in the trusted base</h2>
      <p>
        Each <code>*_model.c</code> under <code>verification/</code> is a
        hand-written transcription of one synchronization protocol, stripped
        to its atomics. GenMC explores those models across permitted
        interleavings on every substrate CI run, so a violation of the
        synchronization logic is caught. What it does not do is check the
        transcription against the C it was transcribed from. Correspondence is
        maintained by hand, and a model that still passes proves a property of
        the runtime as it stood when the model was written.
      </p>
      <p>
        The repository's own audit is the inventory, and it is public: which
        atomics postdate their model, which primitive verifies only under
        sequential consistency and reports a use-after-free under the
        release-acquire model that matches the runtime's orderings, and which
        code paths are not transcribed at all. The claim is about the models
        alone until drift is detected mechanically and that counterexample is
        resolved.
      </p>
      <p><a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale/blob/main/verification/README.md">The verification audit, including its gaps →</a></p>

      <h2>Compile time certifies the structure. Runtime measures the world.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid4" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))">
      <article><b>Proven before it runs</b><p>Reachability, topology, causality, cardinality, confinement and bounds, over every path in the closed world, including the ones no test takes.</p></article>
      <article><b>Measured while it runs</b><p>Birth, dissolve, publish, delivery and transport evidence, emitted by the observation plane and read against the same model the compiler certified.</p></article>
      <article><b>Carried between them</b><p>The topology artifact: a byte-reproducible record of the checked model, signed and attested, so the fleet that runs is the fleet that was certified.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="prose"><p style="margin-top:var(--s6)" class="muted"><a href="/docs/verification">The verification chapter</a> specifies the analysis boundary: what closed-world reasoning covers, and where a budget is a source-level bound.</p></div>
  </section>
</Base>
